  "content": {
    "title": "As playoff rookie, Spurs coach Mitch Johnson has Pop to lean on",
    "text": "San Antonio Spurs head coach Mitch Johnson yells at his team during an NBA game against the Philadelphia 76ers at Frost Bank Center in San Antonio, Monday, April 6, 2026. The Spurs defeated the 76ers 115-102.Sam Owens/San Antonio Express-NewsThere's a good chance some of the Frost Bank Center ushers will have more playoff experience than coach Mitch Johnson when the Spurs begin postseason play next Sunday night.\"I was in Austin,\" Johnson said when asked what his duties were in 2019 when the Spurs made their last playoff appearance, a hard-fought seven-game loss to the Denver Nuggets in the first round.Article continues below this adGet Digital Access and Stay Informed With Trusted Local News.Get Digital Access and Stay Informed With Trusted Local News.ONLY 25¢\"I was around the Spurs. I worked with the Spurs. I was employed by the Spurs. But, yeah, I was probably coaching against Grand Rapids or the Greensboro Swarm (in the G League).\"But while Johnson lacks playoff experience, his mentor, his second-to-none Hall of Fame sounding board on matters big and small, Gregg Popovich, has an overwhelming amount.And Johnson intends to put it to good use — just as he has done all season. \"He's, for me, obviously a constant resource of feedback and thoughts and ideas and someone I can ask questions to, so I know I will continue to do that,\" Johnson said of Popovich, whose 284 playoff games coached ranks second on the NBA's career chart to only Phil Jackson's 333.Article continues below this adJohnson, 39, left the Austin Spurs after the 2018-19 season to join Popovich's staff as an assistant, a post he held until he took over as acting head coach after the NBA's all-time winningest coach suffered a stroke five games into last season. When Popovich, 77, retired from coaching last May, the Spurs named Johnson as his successor.Since then, Popovich, who ranks third all-time in postseason wins (170) behind Jackson (229) and Pat Riley (171), has counseled his former assistant without being intrusive, Johnson said. \"He's done a great job of sharing a lot of wisdom and experiences he's had and then also allowing the group and us and myself to go through it on our own as well,\" Johnson said. \"Our team has a journey we've been through this year that is not the same as any team before us. No different than his many, many teams that went through it. There were a lot of similarities, but not one was the same as the other. Fortunately for me, he's kind of really set the tone of a really good balance of all that.\"And I plan to definitely have some conversations with him in the next couple of weeks.\"Article continues below this adFormer San Antonio Spurs head coach Gregg Popovich, right, hugs Mitch Johnson, left, the new head coach of the San Antonio Spurs NBA basketball team, in San Antonio, May 5, 2025.Eric Gay/APGolden State Warriors coach Steve Kerr, who helped the Spurs win championships in 1999 and 2003 as a player and is good friends with Popovich, talked recently about the connection between Popovich and Johnson.“Mitch is doing a great job,\" Kerr said. \"I got to know him through USA Basketball. He came the last couple of summers, '23 and '24, to help prepare us for the World Cup and the Olympics. He’s done an amazing job.\"... Tough shoes to fill, but I know Pop has been helping him. They're a great organization, Mitch was there many years as an assistant and was well prepared. But he’s crushing it.”Article continues below this adJohnson said he also talks to former Spurs assistant coach Brett Brown, who moved from the bench into a front office consultant role after last season.\"Those two guys I lean on the most,\" Johnson said of Popovich and the 65-year-old Brown, who was coach of the Philadelphia 76ers for seven seasons (2013-2020) between his two stints as a Spurs assistant (nine seasons from 2004-2013 and three seasons from 2022-2025).But even without their help, Johnson likely will do just fine in the playoffs, another NBA coaching great said.\"Mitch has been phenomenal,\" said the Milwaukee Bucks' Doc Rivers, who was recently selected to the Hall of Fame and ranks fourth all-time in playoff wins with 114.Article continues below this ad\"I don't think he's getting enough credit. I think oftentimes when you have a great player (like Victor Wembanyama), you forget about getting everybody to do all the other things, and Mitch has done that.\"Johnson desperately wants to follow in Popovich's footsteps as a successful playoff coach, saying he feels like he has a \"responsibility, obligation, duty\" to help the franchise \"get back to the level of competitiveness we had established for quite some time.\"Popovich led the Spurs to five NBA titles and six appearances in the Finals in his 29 seasons as head coach. The Spurs made a record-tying run 22 consecutive postseason appearances under Popovich from 1998-2019. \"Not being a part of that time put probably more of an onus on myself to make sure I did my part in trying to carry my weight,\" Johnson said. Article continues below this adAll-Star guard De'Aaron Fox said his confidence level in Johnson is \"extremely high.\"\"Not just in him, but our coaching staff as a whole,\" Fox said.Fox specifically pointed to first-year Spurs assistant coaches Sean Sweeney, who was part of Jason Kidd's Dallas Mavericks staff that reached the NBA Finals in 2024, and Corliss Williamson, who helped Detroit win a championship as a player in 2004.Article continues below this ad\"They've been great,\" Fox said. \"We've had guys that have complained a little bit about how much film we're watching, but at the end of the day, I think they keep us extremely prepared. I know we're going to be ready for whoever we end up playing. I know (the preparation) is going to be extremely thorough. We'll probably watch more film than we've watched all season and that's saying something.\"I have extreme confidence in them. And that was from the moment we stepped in, even before training camp.\"San Antonio Express News LogoTake your fandom to the next level with breaking news, in-depth reports and expert analysis.",
